  "editorial": {
    "status": "working-edition",
    "notes": "The two absolutions the priest pronounces after the ministers' confession, each with its Amen. The variant is ministrorum for the same reason as ordinarium.confiteor: this is the branch of the dialogue in which the ministers have just confessed, so the priest says vestri and vestris. After his own confession the ministers answer him with the same formula in the singular (Misereátur tui... peccátis tuis... perdúcat te), which belongs with the priest's Confíteor and stands as ordinarium.misereatur-tui — see the seam noted at ordinarium.confiteor-sacerdotis. Both witnesses print a cross glyph in the Indulgéntiam — do inside the word, the hand missal before it; it is stripped as elsewhere in the ordinarium. VESTRI at w002 is the genitive of the pronoun vos, not the possessive adjective that supplies w008: misereor governs the genitive of the person pitied, and the two forms fall together in the possessive's paradigm — the note carries the distinction.",
    "source": {
      "method": "Verified by collation against the independent witnesses below: zero substantive divergence. Accidental variants (punctuation) adjudicated in the apparatus.",
      "witnesses": "witnesses/ordinarium.misereatur/",
